1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
In what I'm going to call a highly stylized theoretical setting.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Highly stylized theoretical setting.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:17,000
I don't think I've ever seen this as an abbreviation, but HSTS.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:19,000
Highly stylized theoretical setting.

5
00:00:19,000 --> 00:00:23,000
There's two standard examples of highly stylized theoretical settings.

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,000
One of them students have to deal with a lot.

7
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:30,000
There are going to be eight of them in this class.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Eight graded ones, two ungraded ones.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Homework.

10
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:36,000
Yeah, homework problem is usually a highly stylized theoretical setting.

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:38,000
Assume there's two kinds of people.

12
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:40,000
There are muted meds and blasting bobs.

13
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Muted meds don't like noise.

14
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Blasting bobs do.

15
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:44,000
There's an externality.

16
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:46,000
So you work through that.

17
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,000
So that is one case where we work it out in great detail.

18
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,000
And we tell you this is the world.

19
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:53,000
This is exactly how the world is.

20
00:00:53,000 --> 00:00:54,000
These are all the people.

21
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:55,000
This is exactly what they're like.

22
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:56,000
You know all this stuff.

23
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:06,000
And the other main highly stylized theoretical setting is to be found in a certain kind of writing that gets you a dream job for life.

24
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:09,000
What gets you a dream job for life?

25
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Academic publications.

26
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:14,000
Particularly theory papers.

27
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:16,000
There are other ways to get the dream job for life.

28
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
But, yes, theory papers.

29
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:21,000
And if you've ever read a theory paper, you'll generally know as well.

30
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:23,000
These are much harder than the homework problems.

31
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:31,000
The saving grace is that if you're writing a theory paper and you find that you can't solve your own problem, you can just change the problem and tell it's something you can do.

32
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,000
So that's the homework problem.

33
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:34,000
Someone else tells you to do it.

34
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:37,000
They hopefully figure out how to solve it so it can be done.

35
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,000
I've heard in Chicago sometimes people are just given problem sets.

36
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:42,000
The professor himself doesn't know how to solve.

37
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:45,000
I was wondering whether anyone could do it.

38
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:48,000
And then the students suffer and shed and cry.

39
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:50,000
And then find, oh, what do you know?

40
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:54,000
When someone proved in 1973 this problem can't be solved.

41
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Oh, well.

42
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:56,000
Sorry.

43
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:58,000
You had a bad weekend?

44
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Tough luck in Chicago.

45
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Alright.

46
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:09,000
Those are cases where you get these highly stylized theoretical settings.

47
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,000
And in these highly stylized theoretical settings, great improvements are conceivable.

48
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:23,000
So if I lay out a very simple world in a homework problem or in a paper, then you can go and work through the math and say, okay, look, here's a brain improvement I'm going to propose.

49
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:24,000
What would be an example?

50
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
How about a world where everyone has identical preferences and endowments?

51
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:30,000
It's a nice, simple world, isn't it?

52
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
And that's one where it's not hard at all to propose brain improvements.

53
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:42,000
For example, if everyone has identical preferences and endowments and everyone is polluting and everyone is suffering, then you might say, hey, how about this?

54
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Let's impose a per unit of pollution tax of a certain amount and let's take the proceeds and give them back to everybody as a lump sum.

55
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:58,000
And that is a classic homework problem where you can prove that you have tax people and yet the tax is a great improvement.

56
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:01,000
In fact, everybody ends up with the same amount of money.

57
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:08,000
All you change are the marginal incentives where, of course, people don't like that it's more costly for themselves to pollute, but they do like that the air is cleaner.

58
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:19,000
And in a homework problem you can work it all out perfectly where, aha, I figured out not only the brain improvement, maybe you can actually figure out the brain vision solution.

59
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Here is exactly what the brain vision tax would be, which would get the pollution down to the optimal level.

60
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:25,000
All right.

61
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:29,000
So that's where you can get a brain improvement.

62
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:35,000
Now, has anyone ever been on a planet where everyone had identical preferences and endowments?

63
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:37,000
What about a world with just two kinds of people?

64
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:39,000
Anyone been to a three?

65
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:41,000
A world with exactly three kinds of people.

66
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:42,000
All right.

67
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:44,000
So, yes, I'm unaware of these.

68
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:50,000
And actually, technically, even that wouldn't be good enough because the Pareto is about everyone, everyone.

69
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:52,000
And so normally it's going to be every human.

70
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:55,000
It's not even worrying about non-human preferences.

71
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
And those kind of, or if you take a class with Roman Hansen, he's worried about the preferences of machines.

72
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,000
All right.

73
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:04,000
Not toasters, but other machines.

74
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:07,000
It's a little hazy where it goes.

75
00:04:07,000 --> 00:04:10,000
He has told me that he thinks that thermostats have beliefs.

76
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,000
Is that a lie?

77
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:13,000
Thermostats?

78
00:04:13,000 --> 00:04:14,000
Man.

79
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:15,000
I don't know.

80
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:16,000
I don't think he literally thinks that.

81
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:18,000
But it's a little confusing.

82
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:19,000
All right.

83
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Anyway.

84
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:29,000
So, strong argument that when we leave this and move to the real world, where is this real world?

85
00:04:29,000 --> 00:04:30,000
It's here.

86
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
It's outside the classroom.

87
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:33,000
It's on TV.

88
00:04:33,000 --> 00:04:34,000
You walk around.

89
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:35,000
That's it.

90
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:42,000
And by the way, just to get ahead of ourselves, sometimes on the homework or on an exam, there will be a question that says,

91
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:50,000
in the real world, and when there's a question like that, it is not acceptable to say, assume perfect competition.

92
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Why not?

93
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
Well, because it's a question about the real world.

94
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:58,000
You're not just allowed to assume whatever makes your answer easy if you're talking about the real world.

95
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:04,000
If you were to say, well, according to these studies, the real world is perfectly competitive, and therefore, that would be okay.

96
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:08,000
That would be way better than most of the answers that I get on these real world questions,

97
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:13,000
because it's sad to see students that probably used to be able to think about the real world,

98
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:18,000
who have been so caught up in models that they have lost the ability, and now you ask them a real world question,

99
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,000
and they start telling you what a model says.

100
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:23,000
Could the model be wrong?

101
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:25,000
Could it be a terrible model?

102
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Maybe all the models you know are terrible, possibly.

103
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
So, anyway, now we're talking about the real world.

104
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:35,000
So here is my claim.

105
00:05:35,000 --> 00:05:42,000
In the real world, very strong arguments that everything is Pareto efficient, and the Pareto improvements are impossible.

106
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:43,000
All right.

107
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:44,000
Why?

108
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:52,000
Because in the real world, almost any change that you can imagine will hurt somebody on Earth.

109
00:05:52,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Almost every change will hurt somebody, and the economists will often then say,

110
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
well, we'll just compensate the losers.

111
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:04,000
In the real world, how are you going to compensate these losers?

112
00:06:04,000 --> 00:06:05,000
Well, look, I'll write you a check.

113
00:06:05,000 --> 00:06:07,000
All of them?

114
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:09,000
Every single loser?

115
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:11,000
Yeah.

116
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,000
How will you find them?

117
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:16,000
Where are they?

118
00:06:16,000 --> 00:06:18,000
How will you determine whether someone is a loser?

119
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:21,000
What about future losers?

120
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:22,000
What about CSO?

121
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:23,000
You're talking about getting rid of rent control.

122
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:25,000
What does compensate the losers?

123
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:30,000
What about someone that was going to move to New York in 20 years, and now wanted a rent control apartment?

124
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:32,000
Is there a fund for them to get some money?

125
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:33,000
No?

126
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:38,000
Well, then Pareto is not the relevant concept here, because they are worse off.

127
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:39,000
All right.

128
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:44,000
So it's highly unlikely in the real world that you're going to compensate everybody, that no one will be missed.

129
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
So let's take a really simple one.

130
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:49,000
Does anyone here have a watch?

131
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
You've got one.

132
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:53,000
Suppose I go and buy your watch.

133
00:06:53,000 --> 00:06:57,000
Now, you might think this is the clearest cut case of a Pareto improvement in the world.

134
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:02,000
What concern is this to anyone other than the two people?

135
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:05,000
Me, the buyer of the watch, and you, the seller of the watch?

136
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:09,000
And the answer is that if you own a watch, every once in a while someone will ask you the time.

137
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:13,000
And the people that will ask me the time are different from the people that will ask you the time.

138
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:17,000
And when you sell me that watch, do you compensate those people?

139
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:22,000
How would we even know who would have asked you the time for the rest of the time that you owe,

140
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:24,000
the period when you would owe this watch?

141
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,000
And certainly they're not going to be the same people.

142
00:07:26,000 --> 00:07:29,000
We don't follow each other around, make sure we're always together.

143
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:33,000
So, therefore, it will be two different groups of people.

144
00:07:33,000 --> 00:07:39,000
So even if the value of the asking the time for your people and my people is the same,

145
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:43,000
nevertheless, we have made a bunch of people ever so slightly worse off.

146
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:47,000
Of course they're worse off in a way where they may not even know what they're missing,

147
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:53,000
but built into neural models is, like, they don't need to know why.

148
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:55,000
All it is is the final answer.

149
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,000
The final answer is like, what is in your portfolio?

150
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:00,000
That's enough to make you worse off.

151
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:04,000
Now, again, this does get us into some philosophical questions.

152
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
Again, Robin Hanson, just to give another one of his fascinating, but strange views,

153
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:13,000
is that the dead have preferences, and so don't mess with their graves.

154
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:17,000
Because, like, they, in what sense do they have preferences?

155
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:21,000
Well, like, when they were alive, it would have bothered them to know what you're doing,

156
00:08:21,000 --> 00:08:27,000
and so you have made them worse off without their dead.

157
00:08:27,000 --> 00:08:31,000
Hmm, all right, but it's a big view.

158
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:36,000
Now, sometimes people go and try to salvage this idea by saying that, well, sure,

159
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:41,000
individual transactions can be Pareto improvements, but a rule can still be a Pareto improvement.

160
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:48,000
So the rule that watches are saleable could be a Pareto improvement over the rule that watches are not saleable.

161
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:51,000
And this one, again, you say, well, in the long run,

162
00:08:51,000 --> 00:08:55,000
is it really easier to find out the time in a world where people can't sell watches?

163
00:08:55,000 --> 00:08:57,000
Maybe it's actually harder.

164
00:08:57,000 --> 00:08:59,000
But, again, remember, Pareto is about everybody.

165
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:01,000
There can't be anyone worse off.

166
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:02,000
So think about this.

167
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:05,000
Right now, this day, there are some people who are going to die today,

168
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,000
and maybe they were going to ask someone the time during their last minutes.

169
00:09:08,000 --> 00:09:12,000
And when you change the rule, for some of them, there is going to be a long run,

170
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:16,000
and they will not live long enough to see that long run.

171
00:09:16,000 --> 00:09:20,000
And so you have made them a little bit worse off in their last day of life.

172
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:21,000
All right.

173
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:24,000
Now, when you start thinking about this, you might say, well, the counter example is like,

174
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
what about, like, horrible mass murders?

175
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
Are those Pareto efficient?

176
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:31,000
Yeah, well, think about any mass murder.

177
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:36,000
If you stopped it, who would you have made worse off in the real world?

178
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:37,000
Yeah.

179
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:39,000
You might say, well, some of them didn't really want to do it,

180
00:09:39,000 --> 00:09:41,000
and they're just following orders, and they feel bad about it.

181
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:46,000
You know, there's going to be some power-hungry, sadistic people around.

182
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
So the common estimate is like 1% or 2% of human beings are psychopaths

183
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
who just enjoy this kind of thing.

184
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:56,000
So are we going to cause our psychopath compensation fund for when you stop a wanted genocide

185
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:57,000
or something like that?

186
00:09:57,000 --> 00:09:58,000
Of course not.

187
00:09:58,000 --> 00:09:59,000
Right?

188
00:09:59,000 --> 00:10:01,000
You're going to get lucky.

189
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:02,000
You don't get to kill people anymore.

190
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:03,000
Oh, what about me?

191
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:04,000
Too bad.

192
00:10:04,000 --> 00:10:05,000
All right.

193
00:10:05,000 --> 00:10:07,000
Well, then you're not doing Pareto.

194
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:09,000
You're not doing Pareto.

195
00:10:09,000 --> 00:10:10,000
Right?

196
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,000
Or you could go look at the old Soviet Union and say, look, things are terrible there.

197
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:18,000
And yet, is getting rid of communism a Pareto improvement?

198
00:10:18,000 --> 00:10:19,000
No.

199
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:20,000
Yeah, come on.

200
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Like, there's a whole bunch of people who lost power and get humiliated and executed, right?

201
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:31,000
Then you deprive psychopaths of their opportunities to torture and do horrible things to other people.

202
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:35,000
And there are people like that out there.

