00:12:50,000 --> 00:12:53,000
So consider that too.

238
00:12:53,000 --> 00:13:00,000
So that's why in the real world there are no brain improvements and everything is brain efficient no matter how terrible.

239
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:05,000
And in this case you might be saying, well, why do we even go and study this concept?

240
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:08,000
Well, really it's pretty much only useful here.

241
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:12,000
And the other thing it's useful for is just to understand why it doesn't apply in the real world.

242
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,000
So I think there's insight there.

243
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:21,000
Here's a concept which sounds good until you realize that it never matters in the real world.

244
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:26,000
And you may say, all right, well, now let's try to think of some efficiency concept that does matter in the real world.

245
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:30,000
And this brings us to counter-haze efficiency.

246
00:13:30,000 --> 00:13:34,000
So whenever economists are actually doing policy analysis, they almost always switch this.

247
00:13:34,000 --> 00:13:40,000
Counter-haze cost-benefit efficiency, they are for all types of purposes the same.

248
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:43,000
Counter-haze is sort of the more academic version.

249
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:46,000
Cost-benefit is what bureaucrats do.

250
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:51,000
And we're going to see sometimes they do it poorly, but still this is what we're trying to do.

251
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:58,000
So since your situation is counter-haze efficient, if and only if the dollar value of social resources is maximized,

252
00:13:58,000 --> 00:14:04,000
or it could be the euro or the ruble or the yen, it doesn't matter.

253
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:10,000
And then a counter-haze improvement is any change that raises the dollar value of social resources.

254
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:15,000
By the way, there is one thing that I forgot to mention, which is, let's see, a couple of things.

255
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:20,000
So first of all, you have your absolute entitlement to attend that rank in the middle class.

256
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:23,000
So I will try to stop very promptly right in the middle.

257
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:30,000
So that will be at 8.35, you'll have a ten-minute break.

258
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:33,000
So if I am going over, please let me know.

259
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
And also we're always going to end on time.

260
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
I strive to do it at 10 o'clock sharp.

261
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:42,000
So you are, I know, physically suffering to be here until 10 o'clock.

262
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:46,000
We have a lot of material to cover, so we do need to go to 10, but we will not go to 10.01.

263
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:50,000
And if I'm doing that again, please let me know.

264
00:14:50,000 --> 00:14:58,000
I'm going to ask everyone to give it their all until 10, and then I will cut you loose, and the effort will be over.

265
00:14:58,000 --> 00:15:01,000
So just try to keep it together until then.

266
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:04,000
I'm also going to try to do the math before the break.

267
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:09,000
I can't promise to always do that, but I do try to give you the math when you are at your most alert.

268
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:12,000
All right. Now we're zooming.

269
00:15:12,000 --> 00:15:16,000
All right. Now, some relationships between these concepts.

270
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:21,000
First of all, so every calorie-exhibition situation is Fredo-efficient.

271
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:24,000
Every calorie-exhibition situation is Fredo-efficient.

272
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:29,000
But most Fredo-efficient situations are not calorie-exhibition.

273
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
And how can we see this?

274
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
Let's go back to that watch example.

275
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:37,000
I suppose that you value your watch at $20.

276
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
I value your watch at $30.

277
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:46,000
Then the people that will ask you the time over the course of your ownership of the watch value that at a cumulative amount of 10 cents,

278
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
and same for the people that will ask me.

279
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:51,000
Of course, my strangers are different from your strangers.

280
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:57,000
All right. Now, if I have the watch, is the situation Fredo-efficient?

281
00:15:57,000 --> 00:16:01,000
Sure. Right? And well, you know, yeah, I'll let them know so.

282
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Now, is it calorie-exhibition?

283
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
And assume we're the only two people who can own this watch.

284
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,000
Well, so if I have the watch, what's the social value of the ownership?

285
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:14,000
$30.10. If you have it, it's $20.10.

286
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:21,000
Now, why is it that if, you know, that as long as, let's see, you know,

287
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:26,000
how would it be the problem if, say, if you owned it, right?

288
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:34,000
So if you owned it, and then at least, for instance, I could go and, you know,

289
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:38,000
pay you the money and then buy people off, of course, in the real world, that's going to be a problem, right?

290
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:43,000
But if we are at the maximum value of $30.10, there's no surplus to divide.

291
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:47,000
So if you already maxed out the surplus, there's no way you can get a Fredo-improvement out of that,

292
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:51,000
even in a very high-life theoretical setting, because if there's no,

293
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:57,000
if everything is already owned by the highest value ever, then how can the value go up again?

294
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
You can't. Right?

295
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:05,000
If every item is already possessed by whoever values it the most, there's no longer any room for a Fredo-improvement,

296
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:10,000
even if other things are, other conditions are suspicious.

297
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:15,000
Now, so anyway, if you have the loss, the situation is still Fredo-efficient, again, in the real world,

298
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:19,000
because there's no way of compensating those people who have asked you the time, right?

299
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:23,000
But it's not Calgary-exficient. Calgary-efficiency would be if I had it,

300
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:28,000
then society values of $30.10 combined, but of course still Fredo-efficient for you to have it,

301
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:32,000
because we don't have any compensation scheme that is workable.

302
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:38,000
Right. Now, every Fredo-improvement, every time we actually have a Fredo-improvement,

303
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
we also have a Calgary-ex-improvement automatically.

304
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:46,000
So we have the whole idea of the Fredo-improvement is that we're moving resources into the hands of people who value them more.

305
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:49,000
So in a particular, if we had made one person better off and no one worse off,

306
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:54,000
then what is the person who's better off, what are they willing to pay for the change?

307
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:58,000
Greater than zero. If you're better off, you're willing to pay something for the change to happen.

308
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:02,000
And if you're no worse off, what are you willing to pay to stop the change?

309
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:08,000
Zero or less. So, if we go and add that up, then of course we've got a,

310
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:12,000
if we've got a Fredo-improvement, then we've automatically got a Calgary-ex-improvement.

311
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:16,000
But again, most Calgary-ex-improvements are not Fredo-improvements.

312
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:21,000
And just take, we'll go through the list of standard economic reforms.

313
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:25,000
Let's get rid of rent control. Is that going to be a Fredo-improvement?

314
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:30,000
No. Someone's going to lose out. Even if you have a big compensation scheme for current tenants,

315
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:36,000
still there's people that would have been able to get a rent control apartment in the future, and who cares about that?

316
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:40,000
On the other hand, is that likely to be a Calgary-ex-improvement?

317
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:46,000
According to every sublime-men diagram I've seen, yes, that's going to be a Calgary-ex-improvement.

318
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:52,000
Why? Because we're moving the resources out of the hands of people who assign low values to them,

319
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
into the hands of people who assign high values to them.

320
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:59,000
Right? And if you're a normal human being, you say, wait, so we're taking stuff from poor people,

321
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,000
giving it to rich people, and that's an improvement? Yep.

322
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:06,000
Because the rich people value it more. Improvement.

323
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:13,000
One of my favorite examples of this is people complaining about poor people selling their kidneys to rich people

324
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
if there is, in fact, a legal market in human kidneys.

325
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:21,000
And my rebuttal to this is, wait, so sick people are getting healthier, poor people are getting richer,

326
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
and you want to stop it?

327
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:27,000
Of course, the normal view is, yeah, totally. That's terrible.

328
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:31,000
Because the kidneys are going from the people who value them less, generally poor people,

329
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:35,000
into the bodies of people who value them more, generally richer people.

330
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:39,000
So rent control, getting rid of rent control, classic case.

331
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:42,000
Getting rid of it is a Calgary-ex-improvement, not a great improvement.

332
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:47,000
How about getting rid of the tariff? Right? Pareto improvements? No way.

333
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
Calgary-ex-improvements? Totally.

334
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:56,000
How about, let's see, letting in one more immigrant? Right?

335
00:19:56,000 --> 00:20:13,000
Pareto improvements? No.


